        "heading": "A cuisine with several centers",
        "content": "Iranian food is best approached as a collection of related kitchens. Climate, agricultural resources, and community traditions shape what people prepare, even when dish names travel widely. Rice and wheat bread are important staples, but a meal can also revolve around vegetables, legumes, eggs, or dairy. A useful introduction therefore starts with ingredients and techniques rather than a single supposedly representative national menu. [s1]\n\nThe distinction between a shared repertoire and a uniform diet matters. Recognizing a dish across the country does not establish how often a particular household eats it, how it is seasoned, or which ingredients it can afford. The historical culinary literature used here describes practices; it is not a survey of present-day consumption. [s1]",
