        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Cooking methods carry history",
        "content": "Steaming, simmering, and grilling create different kinds of meals. Chelow is plain steamed rice; polow combines rice with other ingredients. These are distinct preparations rather than interchangeable names for any rice dish. [s1]\n\nHistorian Bert Fragner describes the growing importance of elaborate rice cookery in the Safavid period, when Iran was ruled by the Safavid dynasty. His account also discusses older urban foods such as thick soups and the influence of Central Asian contacts on dairy flavorings and pasta preparations. These are historical interpretations based on surviving evidence, rather than proof that every recipe has a continuous, traceable ancient lineage. [s2]\n\nThe same history cautions against treating tradition as immobility. Fragner identifies tomatoes and potatoes as later arrivals in Iranian cooking. A familiar ingredient can become deeply embedded in a cuisine without having been present throughout its history. Questions about a dish's age therefore require evidence about its ingredients, preparation, and name separately. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "herbs-and-dairy",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Flavor beyond the main dish",
        "content": "Herbs do more than garnish a finished plate. Dill, called shevid among other local forms, appears in rice with fava beans, herb rice, pickles, and other preparations. The same plant can connect several dish families while performing a different role in each. [s3]\n\nFresh herbs also belong on the table. Sweet basil, or reyhan, is eaten raw as part of sabzi khordan, the herbs served alongside a meal. Iranica's account describes ground-meat kebab served on sangak bread with onion, roasted tomatoes, and seasonal basil. This combination shows why the accompaniments deserve attention alongside the grilled meat. [s4]\n\nDairy preservation has its own culinary consequences. Kashk, a concentrated preserved dairy product described in the cited entry as dried condensed whey, forms the basis of kallajush. Fried onions, herbs, and diluted kashk make a dish eaten with bread. Its historical association with affordable household cooking broadens the picture beyond elaborate rice dishes and hospitality menus. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4", "s5"]
      },
