        "id": "bread-and-transmission",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Food as shared knowledge",
        "content": "Bread making illustrates how culinary knowledge passes between people. UNESCO's documentation of flatbread making and sharing across Iran and several neighboring countries describes cooperation within families, participation by neighbors, and transmission through apprenticeship. The heritage element concerns social practice as well as a finished bread. [s6]\n\nThe documentation includes household preparation and traditional bakeries, and describes bread shared at ordinary meals and major life events. It should not be read as a claim that every Iranian household follows an identical routine. Nor does a multinational heritage inscription establish exclusive ownership by one country. Its value here is the attention it gives to cooperation, learning, and hospitality. [s6]\n\nThe pages in this section separate dishes, regional cuisines, breads, sweets, tea, seasonal foods, and ceremonial foods so that each can be explored in depth. Read them together: technique explains how a dish works, regional evidence explains a particular version, and social context explains why preparing and sharing it matters.",
        "source_ids": ["s6"]
